{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1125920-first-time-dealing-with-the-umbra-as-st","id":1125920,"title":"First time dealing with the Umbra as ST","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-08-07T08:58:33-07:00","id":1125920,"title":"First time dealing with the Umbra as ST","content":"In my most recent game session a group of 3 Void Engineers and 1 Iterator followed a fleeing EDE into the near Umbra. The session ended there and I'm unsure how I should approach the situation from here. They're in a residential neighborhood in a small picturesque town in Maine. What would the Umbral reflection of such a place look like?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-07T16:45:51-07:00","id":1126103,"title":"","content":"If it's the Penumbra/Near Umbra, then it won't look much more different than the mundane location. It will reflect the emotional resonance of the place perhaps? Perhaps it will be even more idyllic than the town itself. A building that is secretly a Technocratic building might look more orderly/monolithic (perhaps like a taller prison building, or something like Foucault's Panopticon concept if it's for surveillance, etc.). A building that is secretly a Traditionalist Chantry might reflect the supernatural resonance of that location. Otherwise, your players might be in for something eerily similar (but ever-so-slightly different in subtle and interesting ways). I would recommend checking out pages 89 to 90 (it's two pages) on the Penumbra/Near Umbra in M20.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12988-imperator","name":"Imperator","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12988_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-07T17:29:34-07:00","id":1126114,"title":"","content":"Alternatively, you can just pull a Stargate on them and have them end up wherever the hell you want. Nice thing about the Umbra is consistency rarely applies.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-07T17:56:15-07:00","id":1126122,"title":"","content":"If it's Maine, hope it's not one of the places of horror in Stephen King's writings. . .The Umbra there would not be friendly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-07T18:18:47-07:00","id":1126130,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baakyocalder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1125920-first-time-dealing-with-the-umbra-as-st?p=1126122#post1126122\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If it's Maine, hope it's not one of the places of horror in Stephen King's writings. . .The Umbra there would not be friendly.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's right. Stephen King made, like, a million stories set in Maine.<br /> <br /> Okay, new plan. Regardless of what PazuzuAxelf does with the Umbra, that Tim Curry clown from IT needs to make a cameo. Say it's an umbrood generated in response to King's work. Just have the guy peek out from a storm drain, and starting talking about how the Technocrats will ALL. FLOAT! Then have it just leave. It'll be weird in an inexplicable way.<br /> <br /> In fact, that's kind of half the Umbra in a nutshell. Weird for the sake of weird. This is especially appropriate for a Technocrat game, whose protagonists have been conditioned to view the world with rationality. Shock them awake, by confronting them with the decidedly irrational. Or, perhaps, the counter-rational.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-07T18:59:20-07:00","id":1126139,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1125920-first-time-dealing-with-the-umbra-as-st?p=1126130#post1126130\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, that's right. Stephen King made, like, a million stories set in Maine.<br /> <br /> Okay, new plan. Regardless of what PazuzuAxelf does with the Umbra, that Tim Curry clown from IT needs to make a cameo. Say it's an umbrood generated in response to King's work. Just have the guy peek out from a storm drain, and starting talking about how the Technocrats will ALL. FLOAT! Then have it just leave. It'll be weird in an inexplicable way.<br /> <br /> In fact, that's kind of half the Umbra in a nutshell. Weird for the sake of weird. This is especially appropriate for a Technocrat game, whose protagonists have been conditioned to view the world with rationality. Shock them awake, by confronting them with the decidedly irrational. Or, perhaps, the counter-rational.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or challenge their rational by having them confront the super-rational. Let them glimpse the world of pure logic that cares little for that which does not make sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-08T06:07:54-07:00","id":1126275,"title":"","content":"Ok. Both of those suggestions are awesome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-08T06:15:54-07:00","id":1126278,"title":"","content":"That\u2019s a good point. I could definitely have a creepy clown cameo.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-08T06:52:29-07:00","id":1126284,"title":"","content":"WHICH Near Umbra?<br /> <br /> I'm pretty sure it's not the Shadowlands (though, if it were, the descriptions on how it would look are sorta-kinda cannon in Wraith, with significant enough wiggle room for a large number of threads of debating the particulars on the Wraith board, to which you could easily add one)<br /> <br /> As for the other two, it totally depends on how you want to run it.<br /> <br /> If it's the Penumbra of the Middle Umbra you could run it as some of the descriptions above... the same but different based on some perception of emotional resonance. In which case, unless you WANT the town to be crawling with dark secrets and shadows of awful things, then why add that? Is that the point of the adventure, that the Technocrats are going to find out that the town is the source of evil madness? It sounded like they already have an objective, the EDE they were chasing, so that should probably determine the nature of the town. Otherwise, you're adding in distractions that might not be necessary. If you WANT the EDE to lead them to Pennywise, sure, do that. Otherwise, you've just added something REALLY BIG AND IMPORTANT that they need to DO SOMETHING ABOUT (assuming they acquire appropriate authorization, of course). In this case, unless you want the town to look nice but be a secret Hellmouth, the Technocrats are probably going to wonder what all the Union's anti-Umbra fuss is about. The Umbra is going to like the &quot;real&quot; world only a little more so...<br /> <br /> Or, another option, in the Middle Umbra is to go for high Werewolf goofiness. Have them see the spirits of things everywhere - little men hanging in the streetlights who change color and chant &quot;Go, go, go, STOP!&quot;, roads made of sleepy asphalt spirits bound up in the webs Pattern Spiders are laying down on top of them, etc.. Effectively, EDE's everywhere, just most of them are doing their thing and not really impinging on the mortal world too much. (Materialize, Possession, Reach etc. don't have to be all that common). But, if the job is to get that EDE they were following, then that's all window dressing and you need to focus them on mission.<br /> <br /> If it's the High Umbra, then things can be the same as the first iteration of the Middle Umbra with a lot more of the resonance coming from the zeitgeist's perception of the relevant locations, structures and objects, instead of a sort of natural, spiritual, elemental set-up. A lot of everything is pretty much just ignored all the time, and the in the High Umbra that would show up with such things being so bland they are hard to even notice (like, say, that statue in the center of town that no one remembers who it is of or why they made a stature of them). Others will probably look fairly normal but would have wisps of resonance that could be dropped into descriptions (that house isn't just a residence typical for the area, with the notable difference of a red front door... it's also &quot;sad&quot; because it's the home of an elderly widow whose children could care less about her, and if they look at it a bit more, maybe they can hear the echoes of children's laughter from times past). Some places are going to be obviously MORE present... the house where the serial killer lives, the house where the new baby has just come home, the local high school (oh my, oh my, so much teen everything), the hospital.<br /> <br /> Or it could be a bit more phantasmagorical and there could be some non-Euclidean character with the past existing alongside the present almost the way all the wizard places in Harry Potter are &quot;just around the corner&quot; or &quot;out of sight&quot; of the muggle world. A street could have different iterations of the same house all alongside each other, each house differentiated by the character of the family who lived there, with the current house being the most present in whatever way (the others are in the &quot;backyard&quot;, it's first on the street followed by the others, etc.).... there could be multiple courthouses, one for the big famous case with all the drama, the other saddled with boring mundaneness of it's day to day function. And so on.<br /> <br /> Or maybe it's the Umbra they EXPECT to see, so, as Technocrats, what have they been taught is on the other side of the dimensional barrier? Maybe it's going to look like something from the movie Aliens with EDE's excreted termite-nest material covering the real world structures, with alien flora and faune. The Upside Down in Stranger Things as a GREAT example.<br /> <br /> In the end, what do YOU want them to do there? That's the Umbra you need to show them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1062,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","user":"Ajax","date":". 08-08-2017, 10:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-08T08:13:58-07:00","id":1126314,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1125920-first-time-dealing-with-the-umbra-as-st?p=1126284#post1126284\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">WHICH Near Umbra?<br /> <br /> I'm pretty sure it's not the Shadowlands (though, if it were, the descriptions on how it would look are sorta-kinda cannon in Wraith, with significant enough wiggle room for a large number of threads of debating the particulars on the Wraith board, to which you could easily add one)<br /> <br /> As for the other two, it totally depends on how you want to run it.<br /> <br /> If it's the Penumbra of the Middle Umbra you could run it as some of the descriptions above... the same but different based on some perception of emotional resonance. In which case, unless you WANT the town to be crawling with dark secrets and shadows of awful things, then why add that? Is that the point of the adventure, that the Technocrats are going to find out that the town is the source of evil madness? It sounded like they already have an objective, the EDE they were chasing, so that should probably determine the nature of the town. Otherwise, you're adding in distractions that might not be necessary. If you WANT the EDE to lead them to Pennywise, sure, do that. Otherwise, you've just added something REALLY BIG AND IMPORTANT that they need to DO SOMETHING ABOUT (assuming they acquire appropriate authorization, of course). In this case, unless you want the town to look nice but be a secret Hellmouth, the Technocrats are probably going to wonder what all the Union's anti-Umbra fuss is about. The Umbra is going to like the &amp;quot;real&amp;quot; world only a little more so...<br /> <br /> Or, another option, in the Middle Umbra is to go for high Werewolf goofiness. Have them see the spirits of things everywhere - little men hanging in the streetlights who change color and chant &amp;quot;Go, go, go, STOP!&amp;quot;, roads made of sleepy asphalt spirits bound up in the webs Pattern Spiders are laying down on top of them, etc.. Effectively, EDE's everywhere, just most of them are doing their thing and not really impinging on the mortal world too much. (Materialize, Possession, Reach etc. don't have to be all that common). But, if the job is to get that EDE they were following, then that's all window dressing and you need to focus them on mission.<br /> <br /> If it's the High Umbra, then things can be the same as the first iteration of the Middle Umbra with a lot more of the resonance coming from the zeitgeist's perception of the relevant locations, structures and objects, instead of a sort of natural, spiritual, elemental set-up. A lot of everything is pretty much just ignored all the time, and the in the High Umbra that would show up with such things being so bland they are hard to even notice (like, say, that statue in the center of town that no one remembers who it is of or why they made a stature of them). Others will probably look fairly normal but would have wisps of resonance that could be dropped into descriptions (that house isn't just a residence typical for the area, with the notable difference of a red front door... it's also &amp;quot;sad&amp;quot; because it's the home of an elderly widow whose children could care less about her, and if they look at it a bit more, maybe they can hear the echoes of children's laughter from times past). Some places are going to be obviously MORE present... the house where the serial killer lives, the house where the new baby has just come home, the local high school (oh my, oh my, so much teen everything), the hospital.<br /> <br /> Or it could be a bit more phantasmagorical and there could be some non-Euclidean character with the past existing alongside the present almost the way all the wizard places in Harry Potter are &amp;quot;just around the corner&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;out of sight&amp;quot; of the muggle world. A street could have different iterations of the same house all alongside each other, each house differentiated by the character of the family who lived there, with the current house being the most present in whatever way (the others are in the &amp;quot;backyard&amp;quot;, it's first on the street followed by the others, etc.).... there could be multiple courthouses, one for the big famous case with all the drama, the other saddled with boring mundaneness of it's day to day function. And so on.<br /> <br /> Or maybe it's the Umbra they EXPECT to see, so, as Technocrats, what have they been taught is on the other side of the dimensional barrier? Maybe it's going to look like something from the movie Aliens with EDE's excreted termite-nest material covering the real world structures, with alien flora and faune. The Upside Down in Stranger Things as a GREAT example.<br /> <br /> In the end, what do YOU want them to do there? That's the Umbra you need to show them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well the NPC used Dimensional Science 4 to create a gateway that allowed them to pursue an EDE. It is my understanding that you must first go through the Penumbra (I believe the technocrats call it Biosphere Space?) We left off with them stepping through this gateway so anything is possible from this point. <br /> <br /> The chronicle is dealing with increased incursions and the threat EDEs pose to humanity. I think portraying an underlying sinister threat lurking in the Umbra will suit the story very well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-08T19:15:53-07:00","id":1126553,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PazuzuAxelf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1125920-first-time-dealing-with-the-umbra-as-st?p=1126314#post1126314\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well the NPC used Dimensional Science 4 to create a gateway that allowed them to pursue an EDE. It is my understanding that you must first go through the Penumbra (I believe the technocrats call it Biosphere Space?) We left off with them stepping through this gateway so anything is possible from this point.<br /> <br /> The chronicle is dealing with increased incursions and the threat EDEs pose to humanity. I think portraying an underlying sinister threat lurking in the Umbra will suit the story very well.</div> </div> </div> </div>Then I'd got for the Stranger Things Upside Down or Aliens style xenomorph hives invisibly encrusted onto local architecture and landmarks as a more Technocratic take on the Umbra (vis-a-vis how much of a danger other dimensions are supposed to be) than evil clowns and friendly turtles (Turtle Wax saves the day!). Unless, they were chasing a rabid carnivorous vorpal White Rabbit EDE?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}